1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:02,606
CHAPTER 4 — SIMON

2
00:00:02,956 --> 00:00:13,583
Actually, someone had already tried. In 1994, IBM released a phone with a touchscreen. It was called Simon.

3
00:00:13,783 --> 00:00:23,964
It had a calendar, notes, email, and even fax. You used it with a stylus. The word smartphone didn't even exist yet, but today it is considered the first.

4
00:00:24,164 --> 00:00:32,738
But it weighed half a kilo, the battery lasted an hour, and it cost nearly $900. And internet on a phone was out of the question.

5
00:00:32,938 --> 00:00:36,957
It sold about 50,000 units, and after six months it vanished from stores.

6
00:00:37,157 --> 00:00:44,078
It was missing pieces that didn't exist yet. Lasting batteries, pocket internet, and a screen made for a finger.

7
00:00:44,978 --> 00:00:49,577
Simon didn't fail because it was bad. It failed because it arrived too early.
